{"schemaVersion":1,"sourceRecord":{"schemaVersion":1,"recordId":"wikibooks-185952-r4517540","sourceId":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa","sourceTitle":"Cookbook:Dal Makhani (Black Gram with Cream)","sourcePageId":185952,"sourceRevision":"4517540","sourceRevisionTimestamp":"2025-06-21T02:43:11Z","sourceRevisionUser":"Kittycataclysm","sourceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=Cookbook:Dal%20Makhani%20(Black%20Gram%20with%20Cream)&oldid=4517540","retrievedAt":"2026-10-13T12:41:34.045Z","sourceCategories":["Category:Indian recipes","Category:Medium Difficulty recipes","Category:Recipes","Category:Recipes for curry","Category:Recipes for dal","Category:Recipes using asafoetida","Category:Recipes using chile","Category:Recipes using cilantro","Category:Recipes using cinnamon stick","Category:Recipes using clarified butter","Category:Recipes using clove","Category:Recipes using cream","Category:Recipes using fresh ginger","Category:Recipes using kidney bean","Category:Recipes with images","Category:Side dish recipes"],"license":{"id":"wikibooks-cookbook-cc-by-sa-4.0","name":"Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-SA 4.0); Wikibooks text is generally dual-licensed with GFDL unless otherwise noted","evidenceUrl":"https://en.wikibooks.org/wiki/Wikibooks:Copyrights","policyStatus":"verified-license","pageExceptionStatus":"not-reviewed-per-page","attribution":"Attribute Wikibooks contributors by linking each version-specific source page. Indicate that structured fields are extracted and any translated or edited text is modified. Include CC BY-SA 4.0 notice/link and share adaptations under CC BY-SA 4.0 or later.","changeNoticeRequired":true,"shareAlikeRequired":true},"sourceSha256":"2cc3cdda803aa3ebc2c10ec3493b0b038c11b729ce4b73704cde5aa84d626884","sourceWikitext":"__NOTOC__\n{{recipe}} | [[Cookbook:Cuisine of India|Cuisine of India]]\n{{recipesummary|category=Side dish recipes|servings=4–6|time=20 minutes|difficulty=3\n| Image = [[File:Punjabi style Dal Makhani.jpg|300px]]\n}}\n\n'''Dal makhani''' is a delicacy from Punjab, India. Traditionally this dal was cooked slowly, for hours, on charcoal. This gave it a creamy texture. It had ‘malai’ (cream) or fresh butter added to it. When cooked at home these days, more moderate amounts of cream or butter are used. When prepared in restaurants, it is cooked slowly on low heat and often has a large amount of cream and butter added. Lentils and beans were soaked overnight for at least 8 hours and gently simmered on low heat along with ginger, garlic and a few other spices (garam masala). These are then combined with a tangy masala base which includes onions, tomatoes (chopped or puree) or dried mango powder or even pomegranate seeds. \n\nDollops of fresh cream and butter lend the rich finishing touch. It is garnished with finely chopped coriander leaves and fresh cream. It is a sumptuous meal and a staple diet in Punjab and most of Northern India. It is commonly eaten with roti, rice, naan chapatis. This dal is rich in starch and minerals, it is also considered heavy for digestion, however ginger solves this problem and makes it easy to digest. \n\nThis dish is extremely popular not just in North India but elsewhere as well. It tastes very good the following day after reheating properly. \n\n==Ingredients==\n\n*1 [[Cookbook:Cup|cup]] whole dried [[Cookbook:Urad Bean|urad]] [[Cookbook:Dal|dal]] (skinless black gram)\n*⅓ cup dried [[Cookbook:Kidney Bean|kidney beans]] (rajma)\n*5–6 cups water (to cook dal)\n*Salt\n*Red [[Cookbook:Chiles|chile]] powder (according to taste)\n*1 [[Cookbook:Teaspoon|tsp]] [[Cookbook:Turmeric|turmeric]] powder\n*1 [[Cookbook:Tablespoon|tbsp]] grated [[Cookbook:Ginger|ginger]]\n\n* 3–4 tbsp [[Cookbook:Ghee|ghee]] or oil\n* 1 large [[Cookbook:Pinch|pinch]] of hing powder ([[Cookbook:Asafoetida|asafoetida]])\n* 2 [[Cookbook:Bay Leaf|bay leaves]]\n* ½ [[Cookbook:Cinnamon|cinnamon]] stick\n* 3 [[Cookbook:Clove|cloves]]\n* 1 medium [[Cookbook:Onion|onion]] (optional), finely chopped\n* 1 tbsp [[Cookbook:Ginger Garlic Paste|ginger garlic paste]]\n* 2–3 medium [[Cookbook:Tomato|tomatoes]], finely chopped\n* 2 tsp [[Cookbook:Coriander|coriander]] powder\n\n*½ cup fresh [[Cookbook:Cream|cream]]\n*1 tbsp [[Cookbook:Ghee|clarified butter]]\n*2 tbsp chopped [[Cookbook:Cilantro|coriander leaves]] (optional)\n*Salt, to taste\n\n==Procedure==\n\n#Wash and [[Cookbook:Soaking Beans|soak]] black urad dal and red kidney beans overnight in an adequate amount of lukewarm water.\n#Cook the soaked dal, kidney beans, and chickpeas in 5–6 cups of water with the salt, red chili powder, turmeric, and grated ginger until soft.\n#Keep the cooked dal aside. \n#Heat oil or butter in a thick bottomed pan. Add hing, bay leaves, cinnamon stick, cloves, and chopped onions and cook until light golden brown in color. When the onion turns brown, add ginger garlic paste, and [[Cookbook:Sautéing|sauté]].\n#Add chopped tomatoes. Sauté and add coriander powder. Sauté until tomatoes are well mashed.\n#Add cooked dal and water as needed.\n#Mix it well and [[Cookbook:Simmering|simmer]] for 15–20 minutes.\n#Add fresh cream and clarified butter, and let it simmer for 5 minutes. Remove from the heat. Garnish with coriander leaves before serving.\n#Serve hot with naan, paratha or rice.\n\n== Notes, tips, and variations ==\n\n* You can substitute cream with [[Cookbook:Sour Cream|sour cream]] or low-fat [[Cookbook:Yogurt|yogurt]].\n* Use olive oil instead of ghee or butter, and omit cream.\n* You can make this recipe in a [[Cookbook:Slow Cooker|slow cooker]], but make sure that you boil it briskly in a pan for the last 10 minutes, to neutralise the toxins in kidney beans.\n* This dal generally requires more salt than usual for other dal, so it should be added as per taste.\n* Soaking the beans overnight reduces cooking time.\n\n[[Category:Indian recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using kidney bean]]\n[[Category:Recipes for curry]]\n[[Category:Side dish recipes]]\n[[Category:Recipes using asafoetida]]\n[[Category:Recipes using clarified butter]]\n[[Category:Recipes using chile]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cilantro]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cinnamon stick]]\n[[Category:Recipes using clove]]\n[[Category:Recipes using cream]]\n[[Category:Recipes for dal]]\n[[Category:Recipes using fresh ginger]]","isRecipe":true,"recipe":{"name":"Dal Makhani (Black Gram with Cream)","nameAr":null,"servings":null,"servingsSourceText":null,"ingredients":[{"id":"source-line-1","name":"1 cup whole dried urad dal (skinless black gram)","sourceText":"1 cup whole dried urad dal (skinless black gram)","amountText":"1 cup whole dried urad dal (skinless black gram)","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-2","name":"⅓ cup dried kidney beans (rajma)","sourceText":"⅓ cup dried kidney beans (rajma)","amountText":"⅓ cup dried kidney beans (rajma)","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-3","name":"5–6 cups water (to cook dal)","sourceText":"5–6 cups water (to cook dal)","amountText":"5–6 cups water (to cook dal)","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-4","name":"Salt","sourceText":"Salt","amountText":"Salt","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-5","name":"Red chile powder (according to taste)","sourceText":"Red chile powder (according to taste)","amountText":"Red chile powder (according to taste)","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-6","name":"1 tsp turmeric powder","sourceText":"1 tsp turmeric powder","amountText":"1 tsp turmeric powder","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-7","name":"1 tbsp grated ginger","sourceText":"1 tbsp grated ginger","amountText":"1 tbsp grated ginger","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},{"id":"source-line-8","name":"3–4 tbsp ghee or oil","sourceText":"3–4 tbsp ghee or oil","amountText":"3–4 tbsp ghee or oil","amount":null,"unit":null,"foodBinding":null},